WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.667
Welcome. In the next few minutes we'll walk through the seven steps of

2
00:00:03.667 --> 00:00:07.333
responding to a chemical spill in the lab. Follow them in order, every time.

3
00:00:07.333 --> 00:00:11.000
Skipping a step is both a safety risk and a compliance deviation. Let's begin.

4
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Step one: assess. Before you touch anything, consult the Safety Data Sheet or

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.000
the label to identify the chemical. Never identify a spill by sight, smell, or

6
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.000
touch.

7
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:28.500
Step two: alert and evacuate. Warn the people nearby and clear the area. People

8
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:34.000
always come first.

9
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:40.000
Step three: protect yourself. Put on your gloves, goggles, and apron before you

10
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:46.000
approach the spill.

11
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.500
Step four: contain. Ring the spill with absorbent, working from the outside in,

12
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:57.000
and keep it away from drains.

13
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.500
Step five: use the correct kit. Match the spill kit to the chemical. If it's

14
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:08.000
water-reactive, use a dry kit, never water.

15
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.000
Step six: dispose. Place the waste only in the labelled hazardous-waste

16
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:18.000
container, never the general bin.

17
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Step seven: document. Complete the incident log and notify your supervisor. The

18
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.000
record closes the loop and keeps you compliant.

19
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Seven steps, in order, every time. Follow the SOP. It protects you, your team,

20
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:40.000
and our compliance.
